WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:03.700
The House of Heroes is about to get a whole
lot bigger.

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:09.040
In the aftermath of the November 12th launch
of its brand-new streaming service Disney+,

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:13.420
the Walt Disney Company has kept its momentum
going by announcing future plans for its various

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:18.060
film branches - including its behemoth subsidiary
Marvel Studios.

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:23.360
The company confirmed on Friday, November
15th release dates for five yet-untitled Marvel

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.840
movies scheduled to open between October 2022
and November 2023.

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:33.880
The Hollywood Reporter had the complete list
of release dates: October 7, 2022; February

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:42.080
17, 2023; May 5, 2023; July 28, 2023, and
November 3, 2023.

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:48.540
This plan pins Marvel as rolling out four
films in 2022: two untitled Marvel projects

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:54.730
slated for February 18th and July 29th; Black
Panther 2, which is coming on May 6, 2022;

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:59.760
and the newly announced mystery movie scheduled
for an October 7th, 2022 debut.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:04.380
It sets the studio up to release four movies
in 2023 as well.

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:09.660
That outline matches up with what Walt Disney
Studios chief creative officer and co-chairman

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:13.429
Alan Horn previously said about content plans
for Marvel Studios.

00:01:13.429 --> 00:01:18.759
Horn noted in an October 2019 interview with
THR that Marvel Studios chief content officer

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:22.720
Kevin Feige would be making three or four
Marvel movies per year.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:28.600
Four years after the inception of the Marvel
Cinematic Universe with 2008's Iron Man, and

00:01:28.619 --> 00:01:33.119
after seeing how well director Joss Whedon's
The Avengers performed both critically and

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:37.480
commercially, Disney increased production
of Marvel movies to a consistent two per year.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:42.820
By 2017, the Mouse House had put Marvel on
a three-movies-per-year schedule.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:48.280
That year saw the launches of Guardians of
the Galaxy Vol. 2 in May, Spider-Man: Homecoming

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:50.800
in July, and Thor: Ragnarok in November.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:57.100
In 2018, Marvel released Black Panther on
February 16, Avengers: Infinity War on April

00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:59.820
27, and Ant-Man and the Wasp on July 6.

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:05.120
This year saw the debuts of Captain Marvel
in March, Avengers: Endgame in April, and

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:06.720
Spider-Man: Far From Home in July.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:10.400
"See?

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:11.560
That wasn't so hard."

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.380
"Somebody get this stupid costume off me!"

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.540
What's interesting about Marvel's new plans
is that the studio has seemingly learned from

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:25.160
its past decisions, playing even smarter this
time around by spreading its upcoming films

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:26.800
out across the calendar year.

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:32.060
The successes of Black Panther, Avengers:
Infinity War and Avengers: Endgame, Spider-Man:

00:02:32.069 --> 00:02:37.060
Homecoming, and Thor: Ragnarok may have influenced
the studio to stick with release dates around

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:38.380
those times moving forward.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:45.360
It may also be seasonally motivated - scheduling
movies for February, May, July, and October/November

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:50.620
ensures that Marvel has one big theatrical
event in winter, spring, summer, and fall

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:55.220
- or a decision made to avoid over-saturating
the market with Marvel content and potentially

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:57.440
causing "superhero fatigue."

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:02.660
With all that said, a mystery still lingers:
What Marvel movies are going to fill these

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:06.620
newly announced release dates, plus the ones
previously confirmed for 2022?

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:12.760
Being the stealthy studio it is, Marvel likely
won't reveal what's coming in 2022 and 2023

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:13.880
for quite a while.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:17.860
However, that doesn't mean there isn't enough
information out there to do a bit of speculating.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:24.040
The only Marvel film confirmed to arrive in
2022 is Black Panther 2, which will open on

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:25.200
May 6 of that year.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:32.140
That leaves three other movies to slot into
the February 18, July 29, and October 7 windows.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:36.640
During Marvel's buzzy panel at this year's
San Diego Comic-Con, Kevin Feige sneakily

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:42.209
confirmed that Captain Marvel 2, Guardians
of the Galaxy Vol. 3, and another Fantastic

00:03:42.209 --> 00:03:45.860
Four film were underway and would be arriving
at some point in the future.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:51.520
Those flicks may be a part of Phase 4, but
they weren't included on the 2020 and 2021

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:56.650
Marvel movie slate - which accounts for Black
Widow in May 2020, The Eternals in November

00:03:56.650 --> 00:04:02.200
2020, Shang-Chi and the Legend of the Ten
Rings in February 2021, Doctor Strange in

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:09.010
the Multiverse of Madness in May 2021, Spider-Man
3 in July 2021, and Thor: Love and Thunder

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:10.800
on November 5, 2021.

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:15.700
Now, it's entirely possible that the Captain
Marvel sequel, James Gunn's third Guardians

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:20.000
of the Galaxy movie, and a Fantastic Four
reboot are the three additional films lined

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.520
up for release in 2022 alongside Black Panther
2.

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:27.960
The first two projects are in development
as of this writing, and with Disney's acquisition

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:33.840
of Fox in March 2019, the studio will undoubtedly
want to fold its newly bought characters into

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.480
the fabric of the MCU.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:40.060
On the other hand, it's also plausible that
Peyton Reed's Ant-Man 3 or the Mahershala

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:45.000
Ali -led Blade reboot could be the fourth
movie Marvel has slated for 2022.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:50.380
Like Captain Marvel 2 and Guardians of the
Galaxy Vol. 3, Ant-Man 3 and the new Blade

00:04:50.389 --> 00:04:55.240
movie are officially in the works - while
nothing official regarding a Fantastic Four

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:56.880
flick has been announced just yet.

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:02.120
Additionally, Captain Marvel will probably
need a third flick after the sequel, as will

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:04.000
Black Panther and Doctor Strange.

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:08.140
Heck, Blade may even require a second and
third follow-up flick depending on how well

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:09.200
the reboot is received.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:14.400
These are all possible projects Marvel is
cooking up and planning to get out into cinemas

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:15.460
in 2023.

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:20.640
At the end of the day, though, no one can
say with complete certainty what new MCU installments

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:23.160
are coming throughout 2022 and 2023.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:28.200
Marvel is clearly hoping to continue dominating
the box office and remaining at the epicenter

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:32.780
of pop culture consciousness, and these five
new mystery movies are just another boon in

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:34.460
a long list for the studio.

